  "slug": "injury-cord-c274",
  "summary": "Single-cell, spatial and perturbation studies now map glial and inflammatory responses after spinal cord injury and during aging.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is glial and immune cell state in the spinal cord and related sensory tissue. Two rat studies address injury directly: a single-nucleus and spatial atlas of photoactivated cAMP-driven repair, and a comparison of contusion and bilateral dorsal hemisection models tracking neuroinflammation dynamics. A spatially resolved multi-omic atlas links aging and senescence markers to specific cell types in mouse and human spinal cord. Microglial regulation is examined in an isogenic stem cell model, where NGLY1 deficiency raises RUNX1 and alters development, phagocytosis and cytokine expression, and with an arrayed Perturb-seq platform. A pelvic pain study profiles sensory satellite glia, reporting sex-specific translatomes with mitochondrial and vesicular gene changes. The novelty lies in applying cell-type resolved, spatial and perturbational methods across injury, aging and glial contexts.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
